---
title: Stashing
order: 5
description: Stash, pop, list, drop, and apply
tags: [git, stash, push, pop, apply, drop, wip]
---

Stash temporarily shelves working tree changes so you can switch context
without committing half-done work.

@code bash
# Stash current changes
git stash                        # stash tracked modified files
git stash push                   # same as above (explicit form)
git stash push -m "wip: auth"   # with descriptive message
git stash -u                     # include untracked files
git stash -a                     # include untracked + ignored files
git stash push -m "partial" -- file1.go file2.go  # specific files
git stash -p                     # interactively select hunks

@text
Each stash is stored on a stack. The most recent is stash@{0}.

@code bash
# List stashes
git stash list
# stash@{0}: On main: wip: auth
# stash@{1}: WIP on feature: abc1234 previous message

# Show stash contents
git stash show                   # stat summary of latest stash
git stash show -p                # full diff of latest stash
git stash show stash@{2}         # specific stash
git stash show -p stash@{1}     # full diff of specific stash

@text
Apply restores stash changes to the working tree. Pop applies and
removes the stash; apply keeps it for reuse.

@code bash
# Restore stashed changes
git stash pop                    # apply latest + remove from stack
git stash pop stash@{2}         # apply specific stash + remove
git stash apply                  # apply latest, keep on stack
git stash apply stash@{1}       # apply specific, keep on stack

# Apply and recreate index state
git stash pop --index            # restore staged files as staged

@text
Cleaning up old stashes.

@code bash
# Remove stashes
git stash drop                   # remove latest stash
git stash drop stash@{3}        # remove specific stash
git stash clear                  # remove all stashes (irreversible)

@text
Creating a branch from a stash is useful when stashed changes conflict
with current work.

@code bash
# Create branch from stash
git stash branch new-feature             # from latest stash
git stash branch fix-123 stash@{2}      # from specific stash
# Creates branch, checks it out, applies stash, drops it if clean
